TASK 1 REPORT — RESTAURANT RATING PREDICTION

Intern Name: Aniket Krushna Satav
Role: Machine Learning Intern
Organization: Cognifyz Technologies

1. INTRODUCTION

Restaurant ratings are an important indicator of customer perception and restaurant performance. Predicting restaurant ratings using machine learning can help identify patterns in restaurant characteristics and understand which available attributes are useful for estimating a restaurant's rating.

As part of the Cognifyz Technologies Machine Learning Internship, this task focuses on developing a regression-based machine learning system for predicting the Aggregate rating of restaurants. The task involves preprocessing the dataset, selecting relevant features, dividing the data into training and testing sets, training regression models, evaluating their performance, and interpreting the results.

2. OBJECTIVE

The main objectives of this task are:

• To understand and preprocess the restaurant dataset.
• To identify suitable features for predicting restaurant ratings.
• To formulate restaurant rating prediction as a regression problem.
• To train and compare multiple regression algorithms.
• To evaluate model performance using MAE, MSE, RMSE, and R².
• To analyze prediction errors.
• To identify influential features affecting model predictions.
• To select the most suitable regression model.

The target variable used for this task is Aggregate rating.

3. DATASET DESCRIPTION

The restaurant dataset provided for the internship contains 9,551 restaurant records and 21 attributes. The dataset includes information related to restaurant identity, location, cuisine, pricing, services, ratings, and popularity.

Important attributes include Restaurant ID, Restaurant Name, Country Code, City, Address, Locality, Locality Verbose, Longitude, Latitude, Cuisines, Average Cost for two, Currency, Has Table booking, Has Online delivery, Is delivering now, Switch to order menu, Price range, Aggregate rating, Rating color, Rating text, and Votes.

The dataset was explored and cleaned before developing the machine learning model.

4. DATA PREPROCESSING

Exploratory data analysis was performed to identify missing values, duplicate records, invalid values, and unusual observations in the dataset.

The Cuisines column contained a small number of missing values. These values were handled as part of the data-cleaning process.

Duplicate records were checked and no duplicate rows were found.

The dataset also contained restaurants with an Aggregate rating of 0. These restaurants were associated with the "Not rated" category. Since the objective of this task is to predict an observed restaurant rating, unrated restaurants were excluded from the regression dataset.

Some zero values in Average Cost for two were also treated as missing values and handled during preprocessing.

5. FEATURE ENGINEERING

A Primary Cuisine feature was created from the first cuisine listed in the Cuisines column.

For example, a restaurant with:

North Indian, Chinese

was represented using:

North Indian

as its Primary Cuisine.

The final predictive features consisted of numerical and categorical restaurant attributes.

Numerical Features:

• Average Cost for two
• Price range
• Votes
• Latitude
• Longitude

Categorical Features:

• Country Code
• City
• Has Table booking
• Has Online delivery
• Is delivering now
• Primary Cuisine

These features represent restaurant pricing, location, services, popularity, and cuisine information.

6. TARGET LEAKAGE PREVENTION

The target variable for this task is Aggregate rating.

The following columns were excluded from the predictive feature set:

• Aggregate rating
• Rating text
• Rating color

Aggregate rating was excluded because it is the target variable itself.

Rating text and Rating color were excluded because they are derived from the restaurant rating and could introduce target leakage.

This ensures that the models learn from independent restaurant characteristics rather than information derived from the value they are required to predict.

7. TRAIN-TEST SPLIT

The rated restaurant dataset was divided into training and testing subsets using an 80:20 split.

The training dataset was used to fit the regression models, while the testing dataset was kept separate for evaluating model performance on unseen observations.

A fixed random state of 42 was used to ensure reproducibility of the experiment.

8. DATA PREPROCESSING PIPELINE

Separate preprocessing procedures were used for numerical and categorical variables.

Numerical features were processed using median imputation for missing values followed by standardization using StandardScaler.

Categorical features were processed using most-frequent-value imputation followed by one-hot encoding.

The preprocessing steps were implemented using Scikit-learn Pipeline and ColumnTransformer.

This approach ensures that preprocessing is consistently applied during model training and prediction while reducing the risk of data leakage.

9. MACHINE LEARNING MODELS

Three regression algorithms were trained and evaluated.

9.1 Linear Regression

Linear Regression was used as a baseline model to establish a simple relationship between the selected restaurant attributes and the aggregate rating.

9.2 Decision Tree Regression

Decision Tree Regression was used to model nonlinear relationships between restaurant characteristics and ratings.

9.3 Random Forest Regression

Random Forest Regression combines multiple decision trees and is capable of modeling more complex relationships between input features and the target variable.

10. MODEL EVALUATION METRICS

The models were evaluated using Mean Absolute Error (MAE), Mean Squared Error (MSE), Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), and R² Score.

Mean Absolute Error (MAE) represents the average absolute difference between actual and predicted ratings. Lower MAE indicates smaller prediction errors.

Mean Squared Error (MSE) represents the average squared prediction error. Larger errors receive greater importance because the errors are squared.

Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) is the square root of MSE and is expressed in the same unit as the restaurant rating. Lower RMSE indicates better prediction performance.

R² Score measures the proportion of variation in the target variable explained by the model. Higher R² indicates better explanatory performance.

11. MODEL COMPARISON

The three regression models were evaluated using the held-out test dataset.

The results obtained were:

Model	MAE	MSE	RMSE	R²
Linear Regression	0.3036	0.1506	0.3881	0.5130
Decision Tree Regression	0.2747	0.1443	0.3799	0.5333
Random Forest Regression	0.2425	0.1076	0.3280	0.6522

Among the three evaluated models, Random Forest Regression achieved the best overall performance.

It obtained the lowest MAE of 0.2425, the lowest MSE of 0.1076, the lowest RMSE of 0.3280, and the highest R² score of 0.6522.

Therefore, Random Forest Regression was selected as the final model for the restaurant rating prediction task.

12. MODEL PERFORMANCE INTERPRETATION

Linear Regression achieved an R² score of 0.5130, indicating that it explained approximately 51.30% of the variation in the test-set ratings.

Decision Tree Regression improved the R² score to 0.5333 and reduced the prediction errors compared with Linear Regression.

Random Forest Regression achieved an R² score of 0.6522, substantially outperforming both Linear Regression and Decision Tree Regression.

The Random Forest model also produced the lowest RMSE of 0.3280 and lowest MAE of 0.2425 among the three models.

These results indicate that the nonlinear ensemble approach was better suited to the relationships present in the restaurant dataset.

13. ACTUAL VS PREDICTED ANALYSIS

An Actual vs Predicted visualization was created for the selected Random Forest regression model.

The plot compares the actual restaurant ratings from the test dataset with the ratings predicted by the model.

Predictions closer to the diagonal reference line indicate smaller prediction errors, while points farther from the line indicate larger prediction differences.

The visualization provides a graphical assessment of how closely the predicted restaurant ratings follow the actual ratings.

Figure: Actual vs Predicted Restaurant Ratings


14. PREDICTION ERROR ANALYSIS

Prediction error was calculated as:

Error = Actual Rating − Predicted Rating

Absolute error was also considered to identify observations where the model produced relatively large prediction differences.

The error distribution provides insight into the overall spread of prediction errors and helps identify cases where the model has greater difficulty predicting restaurant ratings.

The error analysis is useful for understanding limitations that may not be apparent from aggregate evaluation metrics alone.

Figure: Prediction Error Distribution

15. FEATURE IMPORTANCE ANALYSIS

Feature importance was analyzed using the Random Forest regression model.

Feature importance identifies the variables that contributed most strongly to the model's prediction process.

The analysis provides insight into which restaurant attributes were most useful to the model when estimating aggregate ratings.

The resulting feature importance ranking can be used to identify the most influential variables in the model.

Figure: Top Features Influencing Restaurant Rating Predictions

Feature importance represents contribution to the model's predictions and does not establish that a particular feature causes a restaurant rating to increase or decrease.

16. MODEL GENERALIZATION AND OVERFITTING

Training and testing performance were compared to examine how well the models generalized to unseen observations.

A large difference between training and testing performance can indicate overfitting, where a model performs very well on the training data but less effectively on unseen data.

The comparison of training and testing performance was therefore considered along with the test-set evaluation metrics when assessing the final model.

Random Forest was selected based on its strong performance on the held-out test data.

17. RESULTS

The restaurant rating prediction task successfully implemented a complete supervised regression workflow.

The process included:

• Dataset exploration
• Data cleaning
• Feature engineering
• Target definition
• Target leakage prevention
• Train-test splitting
• Numerical preprocessing
• Categorical preprocessing
• Regression model training
• Model comparison
• Prediction error analysis
• Feature importance analysis

The evaluation results showed that Random Forest Regression was the strongest of the three evaluated models.

Random Forest achieved:

MAE = 0.2425
MSE = 0.1076
RMSE = 0.3280
R² = 0.6522

The results indicate that the Random Forest model explained approximately 65.22% of the variation in restaurant ratings in the held-out test dataset.

18. KEY FINDINGS

The main findings from the task are:

• Restaurant ratings can be modeled as a supervised regression problem using available restaurant attributes.
• Linear Regression provided a useful baseline but was outperformed by the nonlinear models.
• Decision Tree Regression provided an improvement over Linear Regression.
• Random Forest Regression achieved the strongest overall test-set performance.
• Random Forest achieved the lowest MAE, MSE, and RMSE and the highest R² score.
• The R² score of 0.6522 indicates that the final model explained a substantial portion of the variation in the test-set ratings.
• Prediction performance is not necessarily uniform across all restaurants.
• Feature importance analysis provides useful insight into the attributes used most strongly by the Random Forest model.

19. LIMITATIONS

Several limitations should be considered when interpreting the results.

• The model uses only the attributes available in the provided dataset.
• Important factors affecting restaurant ratings may not be represented in the dataset.
• Restaurants marked as "Not rated" were excluded from the rating prediction dataset because they did not provide an observed rating target.
• Some numerical variables have skewed distributions and unusual values.
• Feature importance indicates model contribution but does not establish causation.
• The results are based on the provided dataset and may not generalize perfectly to future restaurant observations.
• Model performance may be influenced by the geographical and restaurant characteristics represented in the dataset.

20. CONCLUSION

A machine learning regression system was successfully developed to predict restaurant aggregate ratings using the restaurant dataset provided for the Cognifyz Technologies Machine Learning Internship.

The dataset was first explored and cleaned, after which relevant numerical and categorical features were selected. Target leakage was prevented by excluding Aggregate rating, Rating text, and Rating color from the predictive feature set.

An 80:20 train-test split was used, and a preprocessing pipeline was developed to handle numerical and categorical features.

Three regression models—Linear Regression, Decision Tree Regression, and Random Forest Regression—were trained and evaluated using MAE, MSE, RMSE, and R².

Random Forest Regression achieved the best performance with an MAE of 0.2425, MSE of 0.1076, RMSE of 0.3280, and R² of 0.6522.

The results demonstrate that the Random Forest approach was better able to capture the relationships between restaurant attributes and restaurant ratings than the other evaluated models.

Prediction error analysis and feature importance analysis were also performed to understand the behavior and limitations of the final model.

Overall, this task provided practical experience in data preprocessing, feature engineering, target leakage prevention, supervised learning, regression model comparison, model evaluation, error analysis, feature importance, interpretation, and technical documentation.

